### 1.3 False merges by trap class (chosen: 200 ms, triple, session in key vs dropped)

| trap class | n | merged, session in key | merged, session dropped |
|---|---|---|---|
| D1 same cmd+session+window (UNDECIDABLE) | 63 | 63 | 63 |
| D2 same cmd, other session, same window | 63 | 0 | 63 |
| D3 same cmd, far in time | 62 | 0 | 0 |
| D4 other cmd, same window | 62 | 0 | 0 |
| origin (decidable) | 500 | 0 | 0 |

chosen, session in key : TP 300 FP 63 FN 0 precision 0.8264 recall 1.0000
chosen, session dropped: TP 300 FP 126 FN 0 precision 0.7042 recall 1.0000

Dropping the session from the key is what turns a safe fingerprint into a
dangerous one: every D2 row becomes a merge, and D2 is decidable.

### 1.4 Stability across three seeds (chosen config)

| seed | events | exact recall | fingerprint precision | fingerprint recall | false-merge |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 0xc011ea | 1050 | 0.5000 | 0.8264 | 1.0000 | 0.1736 |
| 0x5ec011 | 1050 | 0.5000 | 0.8264 | 1.0000 | 0.1736 |
| 0x11ea | 1050 | 0.5000 | 0.8264 | 1.0000 | 0.1736 |

### 1.5 Fingerprint blind spot: same fact, different subject text

hook `make -j8`           -> Fresh
proc `/usr/bin/make -j8`  -> Fresh
The subject is normalized command text; a `/proc/<pid>/cmdline` absolute
path does not hash equal to the typed command. Measured miss, not a bug to
hide: basename-stripping is a Phase 1 decision, not a Phase 0 result.

### 1.6 Verdict

Exact `event_id` is necessary and free, but recall 0.5000 on a population where
50.0% of all duplicates carry a fresh id. The fingerprint is what recovers them,
and it costs 0.1736 false merges on the chosen config.
